<h2>Reading the notice correctly</h2>
<p>The notice will identify the applicant, the reliefs sought — typically protection orders under Section 18, residence orders under Section 19, monetary relief under Section 20, custody orders under Section 21 and compensation under Section 22 — and the date of first hearing. Two things deserve immediate attention. First, whether any <em>ex parte</em> interim order under Section 23 has already been passed; if it has, it binds from service. Second, who has been arrayed as respondents: the Act permits relief against adult members of the household, and misjoined relatives — aged parents, married sisters living elsewhere — should press for deletion at the threshold rather than litigate for years.</p>

<h2>The first ninety days, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Engage with the application.</strong> Obtain the complete paper book: the Section 12 application, the domestic incident report if any, and the affidavit. Diarise the dates alleged — many defences are built on documents (travel records, tenancy papers, employment postings) that fix where the parties actually were.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>File a para-wise reply.</strong> A bare denial wastes the opportunity. The reply should answer each allegation, plead the respondent&rsquo;s version affirmatively, and annex the documents that support it. Admissions by silence are read against parties in this jurisdiction as in any other.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Prepare the income affidavit with care.</strong> Courts insist on sworn disclosure of assets, income and liabilities from both sides in monetary-relief matters. Understatement is the single most damaging mistake a respondent can make: once a court disbelieves the affidavit, it assesses income against the respondent on inference.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Address interim maintenance squarely.</strong> Interim relief is decided on a prima facie view of needs and means. Oppose with material — salary slips, existing maintenance orders in other proceedings, proof of the applicant&rsquo;s own income — and seek adjustment of amounts already being paid under other statutes.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Consider settlement lanes early.</strong> Mediation referral is routine in matrimonial clusters. A composite settlement addressing the DV case together with any parallel divorce, maintenance or criminal proceedings is usually worth exploring before positions harden.</div>
</div>

<h2>Common respondent mistakes</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>Treating it as a criminal trial.</strong> The standard is preponderance of probabilities, not proof beyond reasonable doubt. Defences built solely on discrediting the complainant, without a positive account and documents, routinely fail.</p>
<p><strong>Defaulting on interim orders.</strong> Non-payment of interim maintenance can lead to the defence being struck off and to enforcement measures. Payment under protest, coupled with a challenge on merits, preserves both compliance and the argument.</p>
<p><strong>Scattering the record.</strong> DV proceedings often run parallel to divorce, Section 144 BNSS maintenance and criminal cases. Inconsistent affidavits filed across forums surface at cross-examination. One coherent factual narrative, maintained everywhere, is worth more than any single clever pleading.</p>
<p><strong>Ignoring the shared household question.</strong> Residence orders turn on the character of the premises. Title documents, ownership by third parties and the history of occupation must be placed on record at the interim stage, not after an eviction direction has issued.</p>
</div>

<div class="note"><p>Orders under the Act are appealable to the Court of Session under Section 29 within thirty days. But an appeal is a review of recorded material — the time to build the record is before the Magistrate. A respondent who has filed a considered reply, an honest income affidavit and documentary proof enters any appeal with the high ground.</p></div>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>A DV Act notice is neither a criminal charge nor a formality. It opens a fast-moving civil proceeding in which interim financial orders arrive early and are hard to dislodge. The respondents who fare best are those who answer allegations with organised documents, disclose income candidly, comply with interim orders while contesting them, and keep every parallel proceeding telling the same story.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
